{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Plan review BLOCK, correctly: the plan asserted an automated gating environment where bwrap's unprivileged userns succeeds, without naming the precondition that decides feasibility or measuring it. Replaced assertion with measurement.\n\nMeasured on ubuntu:24.04 (bubblewrap 0.9.0), running a real write through bwrap:\n- default container: UNUSABLE, bwrap never starts\n- --security-opt seccomp=unconfined: UNUSABLE, bwrap never starts\n- --privileged: ENFORCING, child ran and the write outside the allow-list was denied\n\nSo a plain GHA container: job does NOT work, and relaxing seccomp alone does not either. Both the Ubuntu 23.10+ AppArmor restriction (kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1) and the runtime's default seccomp filter must be cleared.\n\nMethodological note worth recording: the FIRST version of that probe reported 'enforced' for every mode, including the two where bwrap never started — because a bwrap that fails to start never writes the file whose absence I was reading as denial. That is exactly the false pass just fixed under c100-c4f1, reproduced in my own ad-hoc probe within the hour. The table above comes from a re-run with a liveness check, and the plan now requires any probe used here to assert the child ran."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787886060811274001,"uuid":"f367b397-8d23-41bc-8cb3-4f6f1644dc53"}